From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Fexiven <48439988+Fexiven@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 09:21:10 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 05/23] rocket: rk3576: signed int8 tensors and per-channel
 scales

Two independent bugs made every int8 layer about 99 % wrong:

1. The driver assumed uint8 throughout (inputs fed as q - 0x80, weights
   packed as w - 0x80, zero points 0..255). An int8 tensor arrives with
   raw int8 bytes and a negative zero point, so the NPU got raw ^ 0x80.
   Lower an int8 layer as its offset-uint8 twin instead (zero points
   + 128, weights XOR 0x80, bias unchanged); signed graph inputs and
   outputs are flipped on upload/readback. The NPU then receives exactly
   the bytes the vendor runtime feeds it.

2. Per-channel scales: the DPU computes ((MAC + A) * C >> s) followed by
   OUT_CVT, with C the int16 in the coefficient record and s the low byte
   of the word RDMA 0x5024 points at. The vendor writes C = Q14(wt_sc /
   max wt_sc), the word 0x0E0E and OUT_CVT from max(wt_sc); a 4-bit C
   with the word 0x1004 and OUT_CVT from the mean scale left every channel
   off by max/mean. Write the vendor values (rkt_int8_fix_coefs) for
   per-axis weights on RK3576.

<= 4 channel int8 layers on the generic encoder keep raw int8 input with
the CNA CVT bypassed and get a valid 0x5024 word. uint8 layers are
unchanged (checked byte for byte with an offline harness running the real
driver code).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
